WEBVTT

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.850
- It's losing mass at an extraordinary rate.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.650
- How long before it goes, supernova?

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.310
- A hundred years, maybe two.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.240
- Too bad we won't be around.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.990
- It's gonna be one hell of an explosion.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.060
- Speak for yourself, I might very well be around.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.830
- Can't forget Vulcan longevity, Captain.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.150
A hundred years from now, how old would that make you?

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.060
- No Earth ship has ever been within

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.000
10 light years of a hypergiant.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.840
How much farther can we move in?

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.660
Our whole temperature is approaching 1100 degrees.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.660
It would be unsafe to get any closer.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.000
We'll have to run our scans from here.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.110
Tell astrometrics to get going.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.910
I'm sure they're jamming in the bin.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.590
We may not be able to get any closer.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.670
Someone else has.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:51.590
You found a ship?

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.950
Bearing 261 Mark IV.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.420
They must be at least 20,000 kilometers farther in than we are.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:11.070
Let's hear recognize it now. Hello the interference is pretty heavy, sir

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:19.920
Then try audio. This is Captain Archer of the Starship Enterprise. I assume you've detected us

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:30.000
Captain Drennic, where have you traveled from the soul system and you we come from a system called this year more than 25 lights?

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:37.440
from here we saw you arrive yesterday I assume you're here to study the hypergiant my species

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.200
has never seen one up close it is quite a sight have you measured its rate of nucleosynthesis

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:50.860
I'm afraid we haven't developed the technology to do that if you don't mind eliminating the

00:01:50.860 --> 00:02:00.000
development stage I'd be more than happy to help you modify your sensors thank you I'm not sure we have anything to

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.660
to offer you in return.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.390
- Fight us to visit your ship.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.500
Meeting new species is one of our primary goals.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.310
- It's one of ours as well.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.240
We'd be honored to have you join us for dinner.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.680
- We'll be there within the hour.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.280
I look forward to it.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.840
- It'll be nice to have a first contact

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.830
where no one's thinking about charging weapons.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.120
- Tell Chef we're going to have some visitors.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:39.050
It's been a long road getting from there to here.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:46.360
It's been a long time, but my time is finally near.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.140
I will see my dream come alive and last.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.360
I will touch the sky.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.120
They're not gonna hold me down no more.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:00.000
No, they're not.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.430
change my mind

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.440
Trinesium can withstand temperatures up to 18,000 degrees.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.210
We've been using it to construct our hulls for over a century.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:10.930
We must be able to get down into the photosphere of most E-type stars.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.120
We can get even deeper with our stratopod. It's got twice the shielding.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.180
Flying into stars is a long way off for us.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.210
Your technology will evolve.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.970
What's important is that you're explorers.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.120
We have gone farther than any other humans.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.800
You might enjoy going even farther.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.720
- I'm taking the straddle pod into the hydrogen layer tomorrow.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.990
I'd be pleased to have you join me.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.800
I am an explorer.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.670
- This is called vanilla, and this is chocolate.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.670
Give it a try.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.440
- Oh, no, wait.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.150
Gotta eat the cherry first.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.970
This.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.880
- Why?

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.050
- You just do.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.350
- A human tradition?

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:58.540
- Exactly.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.000
- Aren't you going to introduce me?

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.630
Ah, this is Malcolm Reed, our Armory Officer.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.890
This is Tristana, a Xenobiologist, and Velo is a Tactical Officer.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.500
Very pleased to meet you.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.510
Why don't you sit down?

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.480
The Captain did ask us to mingle.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.880
Indeed.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.470
I was just speaking with our Chief Engineer, his wife.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.470
Maybe you should say hello?

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:26.780
See you later.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.000
I'm told you're the Chief Engineer.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.540
- Tucker, Lieutenant Reed pointed you out.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:35.560
Please, sit down.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.720
- Thank you.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.010
- This is my wife, Kala.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.790
- I'm glad you invited us here.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.310
- Are you enjoying your food?

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:44.350
- Oh, very much.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.840
- I hope you get the chance to sample our food.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.610
It's more fragrant than yours.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.110
- I look forward to it.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.170
I'd also like to get a peek at your engine room.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.690
- By all means.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.890
- Trip Tucker, welcome aboard.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:05:59.350
- Hello.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.530
It has no name. It's our co-genitor.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.320
Cogenitor.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.860
We're hoping to have a baby.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:14.390
Ah, our warp core emits Omicron radiation.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:17.760
I'd recommend you speak with your doctor about an inoculation.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.380
Sure thing.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:26.290
This should do it.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.000
So, you've heard of these co-genitors?

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:37.300
species are limited to two sexes. In fact, I have it on good authority that the Rigelians

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.110
have four. Or, what's it, five.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:46.470
So you're saying that this man or woman or whatever is a third sex?

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.010
That's exactly what I'm saying.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:52.090
And she, it, is part of the impregnation process?

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:53.010
Precisely.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:57.500
I'm pretty familiar with how it works with two sexes, but...

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.700
Multigender techniques aren't always the same.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.040
But in this case, I imagine the Cogenitor provides an enzyme, which facilitates conception.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:07.040
What do you mean by provides?

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.700
Well, first the female has to...

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.000
No, no, no, no, don't tell me, I don't think I want to know.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.830
Oh, well, pictures.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:20.260
Uh, I think I'll pass.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.460
So how long is this going to last?

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.510
I might want to stay there for a while.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:28.350
It should protect you from Omicron radiation for about 12 years.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.060
That should be enough.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.180
Thanks Doc.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.670
Keep an open mind, Commander.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:08:00.000
You came on this mission to meet other species, no matter how many genders they may have.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:10.160
philosophy where'd you hear that amlet act one scene five your communications officer was kind enough to provide us with some of your

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.370
Literature I assumed you approved

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:19.290
Absolutely, but I didn't think you'd be quoting it the next day retain most of what we read

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:24.700
And you obviously read pretty quickly after I finished Shakespeare last night

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:30.000
I began reading the plays of Sophocles what do you recommend after that seems like?

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.000
It's like you're picking the good ones all by yourself.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.320
That's got to be ionized hydrogen.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.470
It's generating magnetic currents.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.650
I've got friends back at home who are not going to believe this.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.600
I have friends who will be equally impressed with Macbeth.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.160
We're almost into the chromosphere.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:58.480
The antimatter stream is compressed before the deuterium is ejected into the core.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.000
So you don't need magnetic confinement.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.380
Let me bring up the schematics.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:11.840
Where we come from, Earth, there are only two genders.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.700
That's true of most worlds.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:18.940
I don't mean to pry, but does every family have a cogenitor?

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.970
That wouldn't be very efficient.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:25.200
They're only needed when a couple's trying to have a child.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.100
And when they're not?

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.240
The cogenitor is assigned to another couple.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:36.930
They make up about three percent of our population, which seems to be a perfect ratio.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:40.900
Nature has a way of finding the right balance.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.020
Is the one I met the only one on your ship?

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.870
The only one.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:50.890
Once Kala and I are finished with it, I doubt it'll be needed before we return home.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.730
Would you like to take a closer look at the plasma converters?

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:54.870
I would, thanks.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.560
So does it live with you and your wife?

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.630
We keep it in our quarters, yes.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.820
What does it do when you're not...

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:17.650
It eats, sleeps, there's not much else for it to do.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:18.920
What about school?

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.450
They only serve one purpose.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:24.220
Sending them to school would make no sense.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:29.180
Why are you so curious about...

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:30.000
Humans are very curious.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:40.560
You might find this difficult to believe, but this polymer is composed of over 200 naturally occurring elements.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.840
That's impossible.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.230
I read in your database that you've discovered only 92.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.560
I understand how this might seem unnerving.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.510
A lot of things around here do, but I'm trying to keep an open mind.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.410
will you be gone close to three days the circumference is nearly a billion

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:13.340
kilometers we'll be scanning surface features that are undetectable from

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:17.350
higher altitudes three days is a long time to be confined to such a small craft

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.650
especially with a person you've just met I think I'll survive people are

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:27.120
fascinating to Paul they have a lot to offer us this could be the beginning of

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.000
a beautiful relationship which reminds me

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.720
They've asked us for a sampling of films.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.870
I put together a few suggestions.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.230
I'll see to it.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:37.570
You're in charge.

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:44.000
Of the ship or the movies?

00:11:44.000 --> 00:12:00.000
He'll be gone for three days. He asked that you

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.880
download these films and transfer them

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:03.140
to the Visian's database.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.100
Did you meet their engineer?

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:07.980
Briefly.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.330
Me and his wife are trying to have a baby.

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:11.950
How interesting.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.040
There was someone else with him.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:15.780
They called her a co-genitor.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:17.300
You know what that is?

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:17.960
A third gender.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:19.110
Why do you call it her?

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:21.570
She looks more like a her than a him.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.890
They treat her like a pet.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.280
Kept in a room, not taught to read or write, no name.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.580
- Porthos has a name.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:33.140
- It's not our place to judge the customs

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:34.180
of other cultures.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:35.560
- We're not talking about taking your shoes off

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:37.430
when you walk into someone's house.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.970
This co-genitor is treated like one of Phlox's leeches.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.190
You pull it out of its tank when you need it,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:42.770
then you throw it back in.

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.870
- Trigender reproduction is not uncommon.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.600
- That's not what I'm talking about.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.960
This is a question of human rights.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:54.000
- They're not human.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.540
Captain Archer hopes to develop a productive relationship

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:58.130
with this species.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.000
It might be best if you kept your opinions

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:09.550
yourself. That's good advice. Mating habits are often quite personal. Some species aren't

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:15.110
comfortable discussing them. I'm not interested in discussing their habits. I'm concerned with

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:19.970
the way they treat this cogenitor. They're most likely one and the same. Yeah, well,

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:26.010
that doesn't make it right. It's not a question of right or wrong. You scanned them when they

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:30.000
came on board, don't you always do that? They weren't carrying any pathogens.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.520
so that it would be dangerous to our crew.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.180
Is there anything you can tell me about the mental capacity

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.580
of this co-genitor? Is it any different

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:39.280
than the males and females? I'm afraid

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:41.280
I didn't take any neural scans.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:44.460
Is that something I could do?

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.060
Why would you want to? Just curious.

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:56.030
I suppose you could. Fruit

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.910
and cheese often complement each other.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:00.000
So cheese comes in eight for

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:03.350
Actually, there are hundreds. Maybe thousands.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.270
What made you choose these?

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:07.290
They have the strongest smells of anything we have on board.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:12.700
You said our food was aromatically bland. I thought this might change your mind.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:13.780
Try some.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:21.050
Mild, but very nice.

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:27.330
How about this one?

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.560
The odor's a little spicier.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:34.000
What's it called?

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:34.960
Stilton.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.720
Chef says it's pretty pungent.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.270
Haven't you ever tried it?

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.800
Not for a long time.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.930
Here.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:46.450
I'd say that's more than a little spicy.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:51.780
Hmm.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:53.230
And what's this called?

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.000
Alsatian Munster.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:59.570
Interesting.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:05.870
- Don't you find it sensual?

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:16.420
- Very.

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:18.390
- We visit the Armory later.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:22.350
I'm anxious to see your tactical array.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:25.230
- There's an old Earth expression.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:29.120
I'll show you mine if you show me yours.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:41.580
your father it's hard to imagine the men who developed warp drive on my world

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.090
lived nearly a thousand years ago

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:48.850
surprise your ancestors never made it to earth we don't believe in traveling

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.890
great distances there's far too much to see close to

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:55.510
home well maybe now you have a reason to visit us

00:15:55.510 --> 00:16:00.000
it's not that far i'm sure we can make an exception i'm going to take us down to the

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.520
edge of the photosphere it might get a little turbulent fine with me when the engine reaches

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:18.510
critical mass a mixture of positrons and neutrinos is injected into the chamber watch efficiency's

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:27.240
up over 30 pretty impressive it's possible this technology could be modified for enterprise we'd

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:27.960
be very grateful

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.790
- We don't have any married couples on Enterprise.

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:36.590
Do they give you special quarters?

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.160
- The captain's very generous.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:39.920
- No, I wish ours was.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:43.060
I've got a room not half the size of this one.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:44.900
I'd love to get a look at your quarters sometime.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:46.620
If I could describe it to Captain Archer,

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.420
he might consider expanding my living space.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.540
- Why don't you join us for dinner tonight?

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.890
You did agree to try our food.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:55.730
- Are you telling me you have a dining room?

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:58.840
- We'll try not to prepare anything too pungent.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.220
It's pretty mild considering the, um, smell.

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:22.970
For us, the aroma is far more important than the taste.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:26.110
This must have taken you all day.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:29.640
If I had all day, I would have prepared a far more scented meal.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:34.720
Kala's in charge of the microgravity lab. We usually don't have much time for cooking.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:37.810
Will your co-genitor be joining us?

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.440
It rarely eats more than one meal a day.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:44.530
Oh, so in our mess hall...

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:46.610
Bringing the co-genitor to your ship was an exception.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:50.370
Japan? I was hoping to say hello.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.000
Why would you want to do that?

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:58.040
Commander Tucker is very curious about our reproductive process. Humans are bi-gendered.

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:07.370
I'll see if it's awake.

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:10.330
Which one's the cogenitor?

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:11.890
Right here.

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:13.750
Their synaptic density and neural mass

